# Backend
PORT=8787
COINGECKO_API_BASE=https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3
ALLOWED_ORIGIN=*
BACKUP_DIR=/data/backups

# Frontend — set as GitHub repo variable for CI (Pages + Docker frontend image)
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://api-nimconnect.maestroi.cc
# Local dev: leave empty (Vite proxies /api to localhost:8787)
# VITE_NIMPAY_MINIAPP_DOMAIN=nimconnect.maestroi.cc
# Handles default to the NimFeed catalog address; override only if needed:
# VITE_REGISTRY_ADDRESS=NQ19 LLHP G0ML 37RM 5JJD RME1 GLFY 75PQ 402Y
# Backend: REGISTRY_ADDRESS=off disables handle routes

# Handle marketplace (buy/sell @handles) — omit ESCROW_ADDRESS entirely to
# leave marketplace routes unregistered (default). To enable, all of these
# are required together — the backend refuses to start otherwise:
#   ESCROW_ADDRESS=<Nimiq address that holds funds mid-trade>
#   NIMIQ_WALLET_KEY=<hex private key for that address>
#   ESCROW_SIGNER_RPC_URL=http://nimiq-node:8648   (our own node — see docker-compose.yml)
#   ESCROW_SIGNER_RPC_USER / ESCROW_SIGNER_RPC_PASSWORD (matches the node's
#     own [rpc-server] username/password — see nimiq/client.toml)
# The escrow signer must be our own node, never a public gateway — gateways
# expose no wallet RPCs, and this key should never sit on infrastructure we
# don't control. Local dev: `docker compose --profile escrow up --build`
# starts the node too (opt-in — a plain `docker compose up` skips it), on
# MAINNET (see nimiq/client.toml — testnet has no handle registry deployed),
# so ESCROW_ADDRESS/NIMIQ_WALLET_KEY hold real funds during local testing;
# ESCROW_RPC_USER/ESCROW_RPC_PASSWORD (also exported before `docker compose
# --profile escrow up`) get baked into the node's config at container start.
# Network isolation (no published RPC port) and nimiq/client.toml's `methods`
# allowlist are the other two layers — auth alone isn't enough either, since
# anything else able to reach the node's port would still find a live wallet.
